  "worked_solution": "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\u2207f(x,y)=\\lambda \u2207g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\lambda \u2207g(x,y) \\\\ \u27e8{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e8{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82x,-4y\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e8-4,2y\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -4y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,\u21d2\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-2\\lambda  \\\\ y(\\lambda +2)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,\u21d2\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,\u21d2\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$",
